; CAA tree climbing and property semantics (RFC 8659).
;
; The climb runs from the queried name up to, but not including, the root — the
; Public Suffix List plays no part. The nearest ancestor with a CAA RRset wins
; outright; policies are never merged up the tree.
$ORIGIN caa.test.
$TTL 300

@       IN SOA  ns1.test. hostmaster.propgate.invalid. (
                1 7200 3600 1209600 300 )
@       IN NS   ns1.test.

; The apex policy. Ordinary certificates from letsencrypt only; no wildcards at
; all. Quoting is load-bearing: an unquoted ";" starts a zone-file comment and
; would silently produce empty rdata instead of a deny-all.
@                   IN CAA  0 issue "letsencrypt.org"
@                   IN CAA  0 issuewild ";"
@                   IN CAA  0 iodef "mailto:security@caa.test"

; No CAA of its own, so it climbs to the apex. Two labels up, to prove the climb
; does not stop after one step.
deep.nested         IN A    198.51.100.10

; Its own policy, naming a different CA. The apex policy must NOT be merged in,
; so letsencrypt is not authorised here even though it is at the apex.
sub                 IN CAA  0 issue "digicert.com"
host.sub            IN A    198.51.100.11

; Deny-all: no authority may issue.
denyall             IN CAA  0 issue ";"

; Wildcards allowed for one CA, ordinary certificates for another. Confusing the
; two properties would authorise the wrong CA for the wrong kind of certificate.
split               IN CAA  0 issue "letsencrypt.org"
split               IN CAA  0 issuewild "digicert.com"

; Several authorised CAs: any of them may issue.
multi               IN CAA  0 issue "letsencrypt.org"
multi               IN CAA  0 issue "digicert.com"

; An unrecognised property with the critical bit. RFC 8659 4.1 requires an
; authority that does not understand it to refuse issuance, so this blocks
; everything despite also naming a CA.
critical            IN CAA  128 unknownprop "whatever"
critical            IN CAA  0 issue "letsencrypt.org"

; Account binding parameters, which must not be mistaken for part of the CA name.
params              IN CAA  0 issue "letsencrypt.org; accounturi=https://acme.test/acct/1; validationmethods=dns-01"
